{"name":"Middle Ditch","hex":"#7c6942","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"middle","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A centre, midpoint.","The part between the beginning and the end."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Located in the middle; in between.","Central."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/middle"},{"term":"ditch","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A trench; a long, shallow indentation, as for irrigation or drainage.","A raised bank of earth and the hedgerow on top."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To discard or abandon; to stop associating with (someone).","To deliberately crash-land an airplane on water."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ditch"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ditch","blocks":[{"text":"A ditch is a small-to-moderate size trench created to channel water. A ditch can be used for drainage, to drain water from low-lying areas, alongside roadways or fields, or to channel water from a more distant source for plant irrigation. Ditches are commonly seen around farmland, especially in areas that have required drainage, such as The Fens in eastern England and much of the Netherlands."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Sloot.JPG/330px-Sloot.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ditch"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Sloot.JPG/500px-Sloot.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Sloot.JPG/500px-Sloot.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A ditch in the Netherlands","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASloot.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Ouse_Ditches.JPG/500px-Ouse_Ditches.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=t